WEBVTT

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.947
Run this multi-CTE query and read it stage by stage.

00:00:02.947 --> 00:00:07.762
Admission counts has one row per patient with an admission count.

00:00:07.762 --> 00:00:14.132
Patient features joins that result to every patient and replaces missing counts with zero.

00:00:14.132 --> 00:00:16.212
The final SELECT classifies repeat versus single or none.

00:00:16.212 --> 00:00:20.732
Notice how naming intermediate results makes the logic easier to discuss.

00:00:20.732 --> 00:00:24.097
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:24.097 --> 00:00:30.887
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:30.887 --> 00:00:32.542
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:32.542 --> 00:00:39.632
In a real setting such as a sales team calculating running revenue and top products by region,

00:00:39.632 --> 00:00:44.272
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:44.272 --> 00:00:46.702
before discussing syntax.

00:00:46.702 --> 00:00:52.400
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
